 Image19]\n\n1 Financial Performance\n[Image: Image57]\n[Image: Image59]\n[Image: Image19]\n\n2 Regulatory Changes - Finance\n[Image: Image57]\n[Image: Image94]\n[Image: Image19]\n\n[Image: Image94]\n3 IFRS/Ind AS update & RBC\n[Image: Image57]\n[Image: Image129]\n[Image: Image19]\n\n4 RoE - Contributors\n[Image: Image57]\n[Image: Image164]\n[Image: Image19]\n\n[Image: Image164]\n5 Reinsurance \u2013 Profit pool\n[Image: Image57]\n[Image: Image204]\n[Image: Image19]\n\n6 Cost Leadership\n[Image: Image57]\n[Image: Image239]\n[Image: Image19]\n\n---\n\nFinancial Performance\n[Image: Image19]\n\n\u00d8 Improvement in Expense ratio over the last 3 years\n[Image: Image19]\n\n---\n\n# Regulatory Changes \u2013 Long term policies\n\n\u00d8 Gross Written Premium reported for any Financial Year shall be the total Gross Written Premium due for the Long-Term Policy multiplied by \u20181/n\u2019, where \u2018n\u2019 is the Policy Duration\n\n\u00d8 Payment of commission in a year shall be only on the gross written premium recognized for the year\n\n\u00d8 Date of implementation extended to 1 st Oct-24\n\n---\n\n# Ind AS (IFRS) and RBC\n\nInd AS (Converged with IFRS) is an internationally recognized as a set of accounting standards that enhance transparency, comparability, and reliability of financial statements\n\n\u00d8 **Enhanced transparency: Ind AS provides a robust framework for financial reporting,** ensuring greater transparency in our financial statements\n\n\u00d8 **Global compatibility: It harmonize our financial reporting with international standards,** facilitating easier comparisons with global peers\n\n\u00d8 **Public Listing and Investor confidence: By adhering to Ind AS, we signal our** commitment to high-quality financial reporting, attracting potential investors and strengthening our relationships with existing ones\n\n\u00d8 **Improved risk management: Ind AS's comprehensive guidelines enable us to better** assess and manage financial risks\n\n**Early adoption of Ind AS wef April 1, 2025 is in the best interests of SAHI players, their investors and other stakeholders**\n\n---\n\n# Ind AS \u2013 IFRS 17 changes\n\nAmortisation of acquisition cost over the period of the Contract Separate valuation for Re-insurance contract.\n\n---\n\n# Ind AS 109 - Investment\n\n---\n\nIFRS Pnl\n[Image: Image276]\n\n| Particulars | FY2023 | FY2024 |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Profit as per IGAAP | 619 | 845 |\n| RoE as per IGAAP | 12.5% | 14.4% |\n| IFRS Adjustment |  |  |\n| Lease and Security Deposit | -0.7 | -0.7 |\n| Deferred Expense on Procurement Cost | 162.2 | 213.7 |\n| Unrealised Gain/(Loss) on Investments | 21.1 | 103.5 |\n| Provision-Expected Credit Loss(ECL) | -2.0 | -2.3 |\n| Provision for Tax | -40.8 | -79.1 |\n| IFRS Impact | 140 | 235 |\n| Profit as per IFRS | 759 | 1,080 |\n| RoE as per IFRS | 15.0% | 17.7% |\n| Impact on RoE | 2.6% | 3.3% |\n\n---\n\n# Strong Solvency & Risk Based Capital\n\n\u00d8 Solvency for Star Health as on 31 st Mar-24 \u2013 2.21x \u00d8 Sub debt as a means to augment Capital base \u2013 ~ Rs 3000 crores eligibility \u00d8 Current Solvency is factor based as a multiple of Premiums or Claims **RBC** \u00d8 Total Balance Sheet Approach \u00d8 Principles for Valuation of Assets, Principles for Valuation of Liabilities \u00d8 Detailed methodology for valuation of assets and liabilities and quantification of capital for various risks and their aggregation\n\n---\n\nROE- Contributors\n[Image: Image19]\n\n# RoE under IFRS scenario\n[Image: Image19]\n\n---\n\n# Profit pools-Reinsurance (RI) - Obligatory\n\n## Trend of Obligatory Reinsurance in India\n\n### OBLIGATORY REINSURANCE\n\n**20%** **15%** **10%** **5% 4%**\n\nUPTO 2007 UPTO UPTO UPTO 2006 2012 2021 FY25\n\n\u00d8 Obligatory Reinsurance has reduced from 20% to 4% in FY23 over more than 12-13 years \u00d8 SAHI\u2019s should be allowed to negotiate RI arrangement\n\n---\n\nCost Leadership\n\nTech Upgradation led \nProductivity increase\n\nTCS BANCS imp